

Commvault Deduplication?

Commvault Deduplication is a block-level data reduction technology that eliminates duplicate data across backups, clients, and backup cycles to reduce storage, network, and backup window impact.

Instead of storing the same data multiple times, Commvault:

Identifies duplicate blocks

Stores one unique copy

Replaces duplicates with references (pointers)

How Commvault Deduplication Works (Step-by-Step)
1. Block-Level Chunking

Data is broken into variable-length blocks (chunks).

Each chunk is assigned a hash (fingerprint).

2. Deduplication Database (DDB)

Hashes are compared against the Deduplication Database (DDB).

If the block already exists:

The block is not stored again

A reference is created instead

If the block is new:

It is written to storage

The hash is added to the DDB

The DDB is critical metadata—if it’s unavailable, deduplication and restores are impacted.

Where Deduplication Occurs in Commvault
Client-Side Deduplication

Dedup happens before data is sent to the MediaAgent.

Only unique blocks traverse the network.

Best for:

Remote offices

WAN-constrained environments

Trade-off:

Higher CPU usage on clients

MediaAgent-Side Deduplication

Client sends full data to the MediaAgent.

Dedup occurs at the MediaAgent.

Best for:

Data centers

High-bandwidth environments

Trade-off:

Higher MediaAgent CPU and disk I/O

Deduplication Storage Policy (SP)

Deduplication is configured at the Storage Policy level.

Key components:

Deduplication Store (DDB location)

MediaAgent(s)

Disk library

Multiple storage policies can:

Share a single DDB

Or use separate DDBs for isolation

Deduplication and Backup Types

Deduplication works with:

Full backups

Incremental backups

Synthetic Full backups

Incremental Forever + Deduplication is a common Commvault design for maximum efficiency.

DDB Protection and Maintenance (Very Important)
DDB Protection

Commvault can protect the DDB itself using:

DDB backups

DASH Copy to secondary storage

Losing the DDB = no deduplication and possible data loss.

DDB Maintenance Tasks

DDB Pruning – removes unused references

DDB Verification – checks integrity

DDB Reconstruction – rebuilds from stored data if needed (time-consuming)

Performance and Design Considerations

Place DDBs on fast disk (SSD recommended)

Avoid overloading a single DDB

Monitor:

Deduplication ratio

DDB growth

MediaAgent CPU and disk latency

Key Benefits of Commvault Deduplication

Significant storage savings

Reduced network bandwidth usage

Faster backups

Enables scalable, enterprise-wide data protection


Commvault deduplication uses variable-length block-level hashing and a centralized deduplication database to store only unique data blocks across backups, reducing storage, network usage, and backup windows while supporting full, incremental, and synthetic full backups.

